<div id="content">
  <div class="piclink"> 
    <div class="rtype"> 
      <p>Now that you've bookmarked Bolex Collector, enjoy these interesting links 
        for something different. Not all of them are Bolex or film related, but 
        your time would be well spent offering your patronage to these fine sites. 
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div id="link"> 
    <h2>Bolex Related:</h2>
    <p><a href="http://www.bolex.ch">Bolex International S.A.</a><br>
      The official website of Bolex International.</p>
    <!-- Now Defunct... no longer an affiliation of?
    <p><a href="http://www.bolex-digital.com/en/">Bolex Digital of Switzerland</a><br>
      Bolex-Digital brings your memories back to life in HD. High quality film digitization services and the best available digital restoration technologies.</p>
      -->
    <p><a href="http://jacquesbolseyproject.com/">The Jacques Bolsey Project</a><br>
      A in-progress documentary about the life and work of Jacques Bogopolsky 
      (Bolsey), inventor of the Bolex camera.</p>
    <p><a href="http://www.bolex.co.uk">Andrew Alden's Bolex Page</a><br>
      Website of Andrew Alden, author of several books on Bolex Cameras. Andrew 
      also buys and sells Bolex cameras and accessories; his books are available 
      for purchase directly through his website.</p>
    <p><a href="http://www.tobincinemasystems.com">Tobin Cinema Systems, Inc.</a><br>
      Clive Tobin manufactures excellent crystal sync and time lapse motors for 
      Bolex and other 16mm cameras. Be sure to check out his page on Bolex H camera 
      identification.</p>
    <p><a href="http://www.city-net.com/~fodder/bolex/index.html">Bolex History</a><br>
      From the "Small Movies" website, a list of all models of H-16 cameras with 
      approximate serial numbers. Unfortunately, the use of serial numbers here 
      is slightly misleading as they appear to identify a specific camera model, 
      rather than the dates used by those numbers.</p>
    <p><a href="http://www.joelschlemowitz.com/bolex">The 
      Bolex</a><br>
      A good site with general information on the controls and operation of the 
      Bolex H-16, as well as tips and myths, by Joel Schlemowitz.</p>
    <p><a href="http://www.intervalometers.com/">NCS Products</a><br>
      NCS manufactures the Timeflow and Revolution intervalometer for time-lapse 
      photography. Both are compatible with any H model Bolex camera.</p>
    <h2>Bolex Service &amp; Repair:</h2>
    <p><a href="http://www.chamblesscineequip.com/Default.htm">Chambless Cine 
      Equipment</a><br>
      Jesse Chambless; Authorized US dealer for Bolex cameras. New and used cameras, 
      parts and accessories.</p>
    <p><a href="http://bolex-usa.com/">Procam</a><br>
      Dieter Schaefer, of Procam in Prescott, AZ. Authorized Bolex service and 
      sales facility.</p>
    <p><a href="http://www.tcsfilm.com/">Technological Cinevideo Services, Inc.</a><br>
      Film and digital production services, sales and rentals. Authorized Bolex 
      sales and service/repair.</p>
    <h2>Film Stock &amp; Processing:</h2>
    <p><a href="http://motion.kodak.com/motion/Products/Production/index.htm">Kodak 16mm Products</a><br>
      Official site of Kodak; 16mm motion picture film and technical information.</p>
    <p><a href="http://www.fujifilm.com/products/motion_picture/index.html">Fuji 
      16mm Products</a><br>
      Official site of Fuji Film; motion picture film products and technical information.</p>
    <p><a href="http://www.spectrafilmandvideo.com/index.html">Spectra Film &amp; 
      Video</a><br>
      16mm, Super 8 and double-8mm film stock and processing.</p>
    <p><a href="http://www.pro8mm.com/" target="_blank">Pro8mm</a><br>
      Super 8 and 16mm Production and Archiving.</p>
    <p><a href="http://www.dwaynesphoto.com/">Dwayne's Photo</a><br>
      Since 1956. Offers Ektachrome (E-6) processing for 16mm, super 8 and double 8mm movie film.</p>
    <p><a href="http://www.yalefilmandvideo.com/">Yale Film and Video</a><br>
      Double 8mm, Super 8 and 16mm film stock and processing.</p>
    <p><a href="http://www.widescreen-centre.co.uk">The Widescreen Centre</a><br>
      A retail shop, based in London, that offers a wide range of products; good 
      source of Super 8, double 8mm and 16mm film stock and supplies in the UK.</p>
    <!-- Now Defunct
    <p><a href="http://members.aol.com/Super8mm/Super8mm.html">Plattsburgh Photographic 
      Services</a><br>
      Martin Baumgarten's website. Good information and links; offers limited 
      lab service for Double and Super 8mm processing.</p>
      -->
    <p><a href="http://www.zerelda.com/internationalfilm/internationalfilm.html">International 
      Film</a><br>
      John Schwind specializes in the sale of Double 8mm and Double Super 8mm film; Super 8 and 
      16mm stocks are also available, along with processing for color and B/W film.</p>
    <h2>Film Supplies &amp; Vintage Equipment:</h2>
    <p><a href="http://www.urbanskifilm.com">Urbanksi Film</a><br>
      Projection reels, cans and film supplies; also sells 8mm, 16mm, and 35mm 
      sound and silent movies.</p>
    <p><a href="http://www.pacificrimcamera.com/">Pacific Rim Camera</a><br>
      US dealer of vintage movie and still cameras, including Bolex.</p>
    <p><a href="http://www.marriottworld.com/index.htm">F. and S. Marriott</a><br>
      UK retailer of still and cine cameras; Good info on Bolex cameras, as well.</p>
    <p><a href="http://www.8mm16mmfilmscollectibles.com/supplies.htm">Phil's Vintage 
      Movie Films and Collectibles</a><br>
      Amateur movie projectors, equipment and supplies.</p>
    <h2>Filmmaking Resources:</h2>
    <p><a href="http://www.filmshooting.com/">Filmshooting</a><br>
      A great website for the small gauge cinematography community. Be sure to 
      visit the message forums for helpful advice and friendly discussion.</p>
    <p><a href="http://www.cinematography.com/">Cinematography.com</a><br>
      Professional Motion Picture Camera People, News and Resources</p>	  
    <p><a href="http://super8man.filmshooting.com/">Super 8 Filmmaking</a><br>
      The website of Mike Nyberg - Super 8 Man. A great resource for the Super 8 filmmaker, with articles, product reviews and tips.</p>
    <p><a href="http://filmisfine.co">Film is Fine</a><br>
      A site dedicated to still and movie photography using real analogue film. Very informative articles! Cameras are also listed for sale.</p>
    <!-- Now Defunct
    <p><a href="http://onsuper8.blogspot.com/">OnSuper8.org</a><br>
      Impartial and comprehensive resources for today's Super 8 mm and small gauge 
      film makers.</p>
      -->
    <p><a href="http://www.super8site.com/">Super8site</a><br>
      An online 'zine for Super 8 fans; available in both German and English language.</p>
    <h2>Photography and Cinema History:</h2>
    <p><a href="http://www.nwmangum.com/Kodak/index.html">The Kodak Collector's 
      Page</a><br>
      This site displays a large collection of Kodak cameras, advertising and 
      Kodak memorabilia (Kodakabilia!). Includes a section on movie cameras.</p>
    <p><a href="http://www.samdodge.com/entersite.htm">Antique Motion Picture 
      Studio Cameras</a><br>
      A collector's site devoted to antique 35MM motion picture studio cameras. 
      Great images of beautiful cameras.</p>
    <p><a href="http://www.city-net.com/~fodder/">Small Movies</a><br>
      Online since 1996, but apparently abandoned in 1998. An interesting source 
      of information on amateur film guages, with a special section on Bolex cameras 
      (listed above).</p>
    <p><a href="http://users.telenet.be/thomasweynants//vue-optique.html">Early 
      Visual Media</a><br>
      An online museum featuring the history of early media, from raree shows 
      and magic lanterns to early cinema. A wonderful site with a wealth of information 
      and images!</p>
    <p><a href="http://www.movie-camera.it/">Collecting Movie Cameras</a><br>
      English and Italian language website featuring a nice collection of motion 
      picture cameras, with history, images and more.</p>
    <p><a href="http://www.paulivester.com/films/films.htm">Paul's 16mm Film Collecting 
      Page</a><br>
      Great info on collecting and projecting vintage films.</p>
    <p><a href="http://www.amianet.org">The Association of Moving Image Archivists</a><br>
      A non-profit professional association established to advance the field of 
      moving image archiving.</p>
    <p><a href="http://www.filmforever.org/">Film Forever</a><br>
      The Home Film Preservation Guide. Tips and guidelines for preserving home 
      movies and films outside of specialized archives, with a focus on storage 
      at home.</p>
    <p><a href="http://www.homemovieday.com">Home Movie Day</a><br>
      A worldwide celebration of amateur films and filmmaking, held annually on 
      the second Saturday in August.</p>
    <p><a href="http://www.charlesurban.com/index.html">Charles Urban, Motion 
      Picture Pioneer</a><br>
      A comprehensive website on the life of Charles Urban, and his efforts in 
      promoting the educational use of motion pictures.</p>
    <h2>Classic Hollywood:</h2>
    <p><a href="http://www.prestonsturges.net/">The Official Preston Sturges Website</a><br>
      Official website of American Filmmaker, Preston Sturges.</p>
    <p><a href="http://www.reelclassics.com/">Reel Classics</a><br>
      The Internet's most comprehensive site dedicated exclusively to Classic 
      Movies. Great articles and information.</p>
    <p><a href="http://www.moderntimes.com/">Modern Times</a><br>
      A well-designed site with articles, images and commentary on the Golden 
      Era of Hollywood.</p>
    <p><a href="http://www.tcm.com/index.jsp">Turner Classic Movies</a><br>
      A 24-hour cable network presenting the greatest motion pictures of all time, 
      commercial-free.</p>
    <h2>Music:</h2>
    <p><a href="http://www.lounge-radio.com/">Lounge Radio</a><br>
      From Switzerland, home of Paillard-Bolex! A non-commercial web radio station 
      with 24/7 streaming audio. If you like what you hear, consider a small donation 
      so they can stay online.</p>
    <p><a href="http://www.parabrisas.com/index.php">Solid!</a><br>
      An online encyclopedia of big band, lounge, classic jazz and space-age sounds 
      of the 20th Century. Well researched.</p>
    <p><a href="http://www.luxuriamusic.com/">LuxuriaMusic</a><br>
      An internet radio station, hosted by live DJs, that plays an eclectic mix 
      of Exotica, Lounge, Space Age Bachelor Pad, Bossa Nova, Bollywood, Latin 
      Jazz, and Surf music.</p>
    <h2>Collecting, Nostalgia &amp; Ephemera:</h2>
    <p><a href="http://www.coudal.com/moom.php">MoOM</a><br>
      The Museum of Online Museums. A list of online collections and exhibits 
      covering a vast array of interests and obsessions. The Annex of MoOM is 
      updated often with lots of fun links.</p>
    <p><a href="http://lileks.com/">Lileks</a><br>
      Many pages full of vintage postcards, matchbooks, advertising and other old ephemera can be found throughout the "Institute of Official Cheer" and "Flotsam" sections of this site.</p>
    <p><a href="http://www.bitlounge.net/retrolounge/">Retro Lounge</a><br>
      "Bringing you the best of bygone eras". Great list of links.</p>
    <p><a href="http://www.magazineart.org/">MagazineArt.org</a><br>
      A free visual database of magazine cover art from the 19th and early 20th 
      centuries.</p>
    <p><a href="http://www.collectorsweekly.com/cameras/movie-cameras">Collectors Weekly</a><br>
      An excellent resource for anyone interested in antiques and vintage items, featuring over 600 categories, interviews with collectors and experts, showcases and much more. </p>
